Debug.Assert(RET.ToString() == OLD(x) + y.ToString());
</code>
In the provided C# code, there is a comment starting with "@@@" that reads "Value of x increases by y in the function". To translate this comment into an assert statement, we need the value of x before the function is called and the value of y, which would be the second parameter passed to the Sum function in this case. Since we cannot refer directly to the variables before their values are determined, we will assume that the variables x and y are declared and assigned before the Sum function is called, and they are the first parameter value to it. Let's assume that their initial values are x = 5 and y = 3.

So, the assert statement to be written would be:
<code> Debug.Assert(Sum(5, 3).ToString() == 5 + y.ToString());
</code>

The Sum function doesn't change the value of x. So the assert statement will check if the value of x, which is initially 5, increases by the value of parameter y, which is 3, equals 5. The assert statement will fail if the value of x doesn't increase by y.

Please replace the initial values of x and y according to your actual use case and remember to call the Sum function correctly to get the expected result.--------------------
Debug.Assert(RET = OLD(x) + y);
</code>
